WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.854 --> 00:00:13.085
- Thank you so much for
your kind introduction.

00:00:13.085 --> 00:00:14.527
I am very excited to be here.

00:00:14.527 --> 00:00:16.640
I have never been at Yale before.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.241
I knew it was beautiful
but not this beautiful

00:00:19.241 --> 00:00:22.825
and you organized this
amazing, sunny day for me

00:00:22.825 --> 00:00:25.492
so I am really happy to be here.

00:00:27.195 --> 00:00:30.612
This title might be puzzling for somebody

00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:34.700
so let's start out with explaining it.

00:00:36.206 --> 00:00:39.912
It really tries to deal with two issues.

00:00:39.912 --> 00:00:44.079
One, of course, is related to
the term secondary and copies.

00:00:45.703 --> 00:00:49.870
Copies are considered to
be secondary kind of source

00:00:51.453 --> 00:00:53.250
with respect to the original,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:57.496
which is the primary
source on which we rely.

00:00:57.496 --> 00:01:01.663
And so, the issue is whether
many copies may in fact

00:01:04.273 --> 00:01:08.356
be a primary source as
the original that is lost.

00:01:09.514 --> 00:01:12.502
The second issue has
to do with permanence.

00:01:12.502 --> 00:01:15.002
Now, thoughts, actions, events

00:01:17.502 --> 00:01:19.998
cannot be frozen in time,

00:01:19.998 --> 00:01:22.388
they happen and go.

00:01:22.388 --> 00:01:25.721
But we can make them permanent over time

00:01:27.212 --> 00:01:30.068
if we embed them in a document,

00:01:30.068 --> 00:01:34.414
and we can do it because we
want to preserve the memory

00:01:34.414 --> 00:01:37.138
of what happened or what we taught,

00:01:37.138 --> 00:01:40.915
or because we just wish
to communicate them

00:01:40.915 --> 00:01:44.832
that is to make them
cross-space not just time.

00:01:46.808 --> 00:01:50.975
And when we do that, in
fact, we generate something

00:01:52.249 --> 00:01:54.332
that does continue to be.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.004
And sometimes we want it to continue to be

00:02:00.004 --> 00:02:02.756
and that's why we have memory institutions

00:02:02.756 --> 00:02:06.312
to preserve the documentary
memory of our time.

00:02:06.312 --> 00:02:10.479
But sometimes we really don't
think about the permanence

00:02:11.971 --> 00:02:13.638
of what we generate.

00:02:14.498 --> 00:02:16.084
We just generate it.

00:02:16.084 --> 00:02:20.251
And then it is there, some
place, and keeps being there,

00:02:22.299 --> 00:02:25.206
and nobody's getting rid of it.

00:02:25.206 --> 00:02:28.373
So, this is the involuntary permanence

00:02:30.742 --> 00:02:34.400
of the many copies that
we're generating today.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.567
So, this is what the subject
of this lecture today is.

00:02:41.704 --> 00:02:46.632
If you think that this is
an issue of digital times,

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:48.278
it is not.

00:02:48.278 --> 00:02:52.445
At the time of classic Rome
the records were written on wax

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:58.188
and they were taken
through underground tunnels

00:02:58.188 --> 00:03:01.441
from the offices to the Tabularium

00:03:01.441 --> 00:03:05.608
where they had to be put
in a very protected place

00:03:06.998 --> 00:03:11.028
because of course everybody
could tamper with wax.

00:03:11.028 --> 00:03:14.278
And then manuscripts would make copies,

00:03:15.753 --> 00:03:17.785
a multitude of copies.

00:03:17.785 --> 00:03:21.202
They were passed on for access to people.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:27.305
Among these people Cicero
would transcribe writing.

00:03:27.305 --> 00:03:30.617
So you see it's a never
ending chain when it starts,

00:03:30.617 --> 00:03:33.990
and it started then and it continues now

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:37.534
and we're still finding
copies of documents

00:03:37.534 --> 00:03:41.748
that were supposed to have been destroyed.

00:03:41.748 --> 00:03:45.228
So, as we talk about Rome, Roman Law.

00:03:45.228 --> 00:03:49.707
Most concepts that control
the way we deal with documents

00:03:49.707 --> 00:03:51.291
come from Roman Law.

00:03:51.291 --> 00:03:55.069
For example, documents
preserve perpetual memory

00:03:55.069 --> 00:03:58.758
of facts and acts to which they relate.

00:03:58.758 --> 00:04:01.958
Authentication has to
be based on procedure

00:04:01.958 --> 00:04:05.789
on how the documents
are generated and used.

00:04:05.789 --> 00:04:09.406
The deposit of a document
in a public place

00:04:09.406 --> 00:04:14.056
guarantees their reliability
as the testimony of the actions

00:04:14.056 --> 00:04:16.964
that the document talks about.

00:04:16.964 --> 00:04:20.691
Antiquity gives documents
the highest authority.

00:04:20.691 --> 00:04:21.524
Why?

00:04:21.524 --> 00:04:23.738
Because obviously they were not generated

00:04:23.738 --> 00:04:26.888
for the uses we make up them today.

00:04:26.888 --> 00:04:29.073
So you can trust what they say

00:04:29.073 --> 00:04:31.714
because they were not tailored to say

00:04:31.714 --> 00:04:34.297
what we want them to say today.

00:04:35.543 --> 00:04:40.016
Unbroken legitimate custody
ensures document authenticity,

00:04:40.016 --> 00:04:42.016
of course, by inference.

00:04:43.206 --> 00:04:45.632
This is the Justinian Code.

00:04:45.632 --> 00:04:49.011
We have built much of it
on the Justinian Code,

00:04:49.011 --> 00:04:51.097
but of course this is a body of law

00:04:51.097 --> 00:04:53.597
so laws are made to be broken.

00:04:54.734 --> 00:04:58.003
So, the rule of law was circumvented.

00:04:58.003 --> 00:05:02.057
It is very easy to go, I
guess, to use those rules,

00:05:02.057 --> 00:05:06.498
by for example bringing the
forgery to a public repository

00:05:06.498 --> 00:05:08.245
and depositing it there.

00:05:08.245 --> 00:05:11.371
It automatically becomes trustworthy.

00:05:11.371 --> 00:05:15.538
So, in the 17th century a
Benedictine, Dom Jean Mabillon,

00:05:18.749 --> 00:05:22.224
in France developed a
discipline called Diplomatics,

00:05:22.224 --> 00:05:24.391
which means about records.

00:05:25.753 --> 00:05:29.233
He basically demonstrated
that you can base

00:05:29.233 --> 00:05:31.883
the trustworthiness of documents

00:05:31.883 --> 00:05:35.753
on the analysis of the
process of their genesis,

00:05:35.753 --> 00:05:38.936
on their form, on their structure,

00:05:38.936 --> 00:05:43.101
and on the way they are
transmitted through time and space.

00:05:43.101 --> 00:05:47.583
So, during those times there
were many judicial disputes

00:05:47.583 --> 00:05:49.358
on the authenticity of documents

00:05:49.358 --> 00:05:54.084
which were called Bella
Diplomatica, diplomatic wars.

00:05:54.084 --> 00:05:57.790
And they were based on diplomatic methods

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:02.604
because, and I quote now the
Bishop of Venice at the time

00:06:02.604 --> 00:06:04.718
Balthazar Bonifacio, who said,

00:06:04.718 --> 00:06:09.616
"It is so much better to
win by reason than by force.

00:06:09.616 --> 00:06:12.593
"So records of documents
are so much better

00:06:12.593 --> 00:06:15.255
"than navy yards and weapons."

00:06:15.255 --> 00:06:19.374
And that's the way in
which the law of evidence

00:06:19.374 --> 00:06:23.206
had origin in the
faculties of law in Europe.

00:06:23.206 --> 00:06:26.084
So, by mid 18th century
all the faculties of law

00:06:26.084 --> 00:06:27.967
taught Archival Science and Diplomatics

00:06:27.967 --> 00:06:32.261
as forensic disciplines, that
is in order to prove the facts

00:06:32.261 --> 00:06:34.928
that the documents talked about.

00:06:36.365 --> 00:06:40.282
So, then, if you put
together archival concepts

00:06:41.239 --> 00:06:45.787
and diplomatic concepts,
you get this field of study

00:06:45.787 --> 00:06:48.195
that is called Archival Diplomatics,

00:06:48.195 --> 00:06:52.362
which analyzes documents on
their own as individual items,

00:06:54.238 --> 00:06:58.405
that's Diplomatics, and in
context among all the documents

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.336
to which they are related,
and that's Archival Science.

00:07:04.336 --> 00:07:09.322
Now, we can use this science,
Archival Diplomatics -

00:07:09.322 --> 00:07:13.301
come forward, there
are lots of seats here.

00:07:13.301 --> 00:07:16.552
We can use it retrospectively,

00:07:16.552 --> 00:07:20.517
that is in order to understand
what happened in the past

00:07:20.517 --> 00:07:24.978
or to understand and identify
the documents of the past.

00:07:24.978 --> 00:07:27.728
Or we can use them prospectively,

00:07:30.232 --> 00:07:32.676
that is to design the systems

00:07:32.676 --> 00:07:35.056
that will generate the
records of the future.

00:07:35.056 --> 00:07:38.989
So once we understand what
are the essential elements

00:07:38.989 --> 00:07:43.156
of the documents on which
we can base our assessment

00:07:44.015 --> 00:07:46.852
or what they are or whether
they are trustworthy, et cetera,

00:07:46.852 --> 00:07:51.019
we can embed those elements
in the systems that we design

00:07:52.521 --> 00:07:55.089
for the creation of future documents.

00:07:55.089 --> 00:07:58.970
So that's the way Archival
Diplomatics is used today.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:01.992
That's why you have
Janus' face there, right?

00:08:01.992 --> 00:08:04.031
The two faces, looking at the past

00:08:04.031 --> 00:08:05.613
and looking at the future.

00:08:05.613 --> 00:08:09.038
And there are three specific
areas that we deal with

00:08:09.038 --> 00:08:10.505
in Archival Diplomatics.

00:08:10.505 --> 00:08:14.868
You can see on the left
the concept of record,

00:08:14.868 --> 00:08:18.426
so the analysis of all
manifestations of records

00:08:18.426 --> 00:08:21.020
through time and form and element.

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:24.076
In the middle, the process of creation

00:08:24.076 --> 00:08:26.837
of the documents through time.

00:08:26.837 --> 00:08:30.427
And on the right, the trustworthiness.

00:08:30.427 --> 00:08:34.594
Now, we want to focus now
on the trustworthiness area

00:08:36.307 --> 00:08:38.057
for a moment or more.

00:08:39.468 --> 00:08:40.950
Can I move this? Okay.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:45.117
So, trustworthiness really
consists of three concepts:

00:08:46.181 --> 00:08:50.073
reliability, accuracy and authenticity,

00:08:50.073 --> 00:08:51.943
which are quite distinct.

00:08:51.943 --> 00:08:56.514
So, the reliability is the
trustworthiness of a document

00:08:56.514 --> 00:09:01.052
as a statement of fact,
that is as to its content.

00:09:01.052 --> 00:09:04.328
It means you trust what the document says.

00:09:04.328 --> 00:09:06.876
How do you access reliability?

00:09:06.876 --> 00:09:10.501
Normally you base it on the
competence of the author.

00:09:10.501 --> 00:09:13.490
For example, if you have a blueprint

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.494
and the author is an engineer
you tend to trust it more

00:09:17.494 --> 00:09:21.383
than if the author is a
janitor of the building, right?

00:09:21.383 --> 00:09:24.922
So, you see, it's again an inference.

00:09:24.922 --> 00:09:29.225
I trust the author, I trust
the document, or its content.

00:09:29.225 --> 00:09:32.638
And the second element is
the control on the creation.

00:09:32.638 --> 00:09:35.450
Not everybody can get into the system.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:38.864
Not everybody can use that letterhead.

00:09:38.864 --> 00:09:42.447
Not everybody can use
the stamp, and so on.

00:09:43.538 --> 00:09:48.202
Accuracy is the correctness
and precision of the content.

00:09:48.202 --> 00:09:52.193
So, it's really the
data within the content.

00:09:52.193 --> 00:09:56.297
And again it's based on the
competence of the author,

00:09:56.297 --> 00:10:00.379
but in addition it's based
on control on transmission.

00:10:00.379 --> 00:10:03.223
Because especially in
the digital environment

00:10:03.223 --> 00:10:07.381
when you transmit a document
from a system to another

00:10:07.381 --> 00:10:09.798
you easily get modifications.

00:10:10.996 --> 00:10:14.940
Authenticity is the thing
we want to look at today,

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:15.773
in particular.

00:10:15.773 --> 00:10:17.894
It's the trustworthiness of a document

00:10:17.894 --> 00:10:19.653
that is what it says it is,

00:10:19.653 --> 00:10:23.969
which is being uncorrupted
and untampered with.

00:10:23.969 --> 00:10:27.848
And we base it on two
very important factors:

00:10:27.848 --> 00:10:32.015
on the identity of the
document and on its integrity.

00:10:33.295 --> 00:10:35.045
So, what document on?

00:10:36.036 --> 00:10:39.583
On which type of document we base it?

00:10:39.583 --> 00:10:43.500
Well, we know from
Dimplomatics that a document

00:10:44.709 --> 00:10:48.401
can have different degrees of perfection,

00:10:48.401 --> 00:10:51.616
and the degree of perfection of a document

00:10:51.616 --> 00:10:54.396
determines its authority.

00:10:54.396 --> 00:10:58.692
So, if you write a note
without signing it,

00:10:58.692 --> 00:11:00.735
it doesn't have much of an authority.

00:11:00.735 --> 00:11:03.721
But if you put 20 stamps on
it and as many signatures

00:11:03.721 --> 00:11:06.180
and a gold seal, you get the impression

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:09.846
that this document is saying
something really important,

00:11:09.846 --> 00:11:10.679
right?

00:11:10.679 --> 00:11:14.846
So, there are three statuses
of transmission of documents,

00:11:15.973 --> 00:11:18.228
only three ways that a document can be.

00:11:18.228 --> 00:11:20.005
Can be a draft,

00:11:20.005 --> 00:11:23.978
which a document prepared
for purposes of correction.

00:11:23.978 --> 00:11:28.739
It is meant to be provisional,
it is meant to be temporary.

00:11:28.739 --> 00:11:31.422
It does not mean that it is not important.

00:11:31.422 --> 00:11:33.577
To a historian a draft
may be more important

00:11:33.577 --> 00:11:37.692
than an actual original document
that was generated from it.

00:11:37.692 --> 00:11:39.835
But it simply means it was not meant

00:11:39.835 --> 00:11:41.939
to have legal consequences,

00:11:41.939 --> 00:11:44.905
it was not meant to attest to facts.

00:11:44.905 --> 00:11:48.738
It was meant in preparation
of something else.

00:11:49.721 --> 00:11:51.598
Then you have the original.

00:11:51.598 --> 00:11:55.409
The original has three characteristics.

00:11:55.409 --> 00:11:57.978
Primitiveness: must be the first.

00:11:57.978 --> 00:12:02.155
But it must be the first that
is complete, so completeness.

00:12:02.155 --> 00:12:04.656
And must be able to reach the purposes

00:12:04.656 --> 00:12:06.796
for which it was generated.

00:12:06.796 --> 00:12:10.311
So why do I write there in brackets a fax?

00:12:10.311 --> 00:12:14.022
Well, because what do you
do when you have a document

00:12:14.022 --> 00:12:17.149
that is the first and it is complete,

00:12:17.149 --> 00:12:19.689
the one that you put in a fax machine?

00:12:19.689 --> 00:12:22.046
It is unable to reach its purposes

00:12:22.046 --> 00:12:24.037
other than by the creation

00:12:24.037 --> 00:12:27.003
of another document on the other side,

00:12:27.003 --> 00:12:30.444
which is the one that is
effective but is not complete

00:12:30.444 --> 00:12:32.640
because it doesn't have
the original signature

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:34.640
and it is not the first.

00:12:35.769 --> 00:12:39.142
And this is the reason why procedurally

00:12:39.142 --> 00:12:43.300
we always require both the
sender and the recipient

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.216
to keep their own part

00:12:45.216 --> 00:12:48.539
because together it is an original.

00:12:48.539 --> 00:12:51.872
Separated they don't attest to anything.

00:12:53.477 --> 00:12:56.939
And you know why, because
the received document

00:12:56.939 --> 00:13:00.514
can prove that was sent
by a specific machine

00:13:00.514 --> 00:13:02.335
at a specific time.

00:13:02.335 --> 00:13:05.789
It cannot prove who actually
put it in the machine,

00:13:05.789 --> 00:13:08.369
number one, and what
was put in the machine

00:13:08.369 --> 00:13:11.173
because I could fold
three documents together

00:13:11.173 --> 00:13:14.471
to compose something and pass it through

00:13:14.471 --> 00:13:16.657
and then separate it.

00:13:16.657 --> 00:13:18.452
So I can always say I never sent it,

00:13:18.452 --> 00:13:21.687
there is no such a document with me.

00:13:21.687 --> 00:13:24.561
Especially when documents are supposed

00:13:24.561 --> 00:13:28.728
to reach legal effects, you
better keep it on both sides.

00:13:30.576 --> 00:13:34.238
So the concept of original through time.

00:13:34.238 --> 00:13:37.720
When we think about the
digital environment,

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:40.092
so may people say, "Oh,
there is no original."

00:13:40.092 --> 00:13:41.817
Of course there is original,

00:13:41.817 --> 00:13:44.496
except it lasts for a nano-second,

00:13:44.496 --> 00:13:47.440
because the original is the document

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:51.206
that is first received by
the intended recipient.

00:13:51.206 --> 00:13:54.651
You open it, you look at
it, that's your original.

00:13:54.651 --> 00:13:55.921
Then you save it,

00:13:55.921 --> 00:13:59.041
by doing that you take
it apart and destroy it.

00:13:59.041 --> 00:14:02.319
And then every time you recall
it you create a new one,

00:14:02.319 --> 00:14:03.608
it's a copy.

00:14:03.608 --> 00:14:06.743
But the original exists
even if for a brief time.

00:14:06.743 --> 00:14:09.993
Now, through the law, the legal systems

00:14:11.252 --> 00:14:15.893
involve the civil law system
and the common law system,

00:14:15.893 --> 00:14:20.139
the original is always the best evidence.

00:14:20.139 --> 00:14:24.139
So, what happens when we
don't have an original.

00:14:25.365 --> 00:14:27.782
We often have lost originals.

00:14:28.673 --> 00:14:32.340
And copies are not just
made for convenience

00:14:33.479 --> 00:14:35.763
so that they can be accessed by many,

00:14:35.763 --> 00:14:38.659
but they can be made for legal purposes.

00:14:38.659 --> 00:14:41.259
So, we have different kinds of copies,

00:14:41.259 --> 00:14:45.989
and what distinguishes them is
the authority that they have

00:14:45.989 --> 00:14:49.042
but also who generates them.

00:14:49.042 --> 00:14:52.219
So for example, an
authentic copy is a copy

00:14:52.219 --> 00:14:55.379
that is declared to be authentic

00:14:55.379 --> 00:14:59.296
that is conformed to the
original by an officer

00:15:00.183 --> 00:15:02.330
who has that responsibility.

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:03.387
Cannot be you.

00:15:03.387 --> 00:15:08.080
It has got to be a notary,
a lawyer, a professional,

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:12.062
it has to be somebody else, a third party.

00:15:12.062 --> 00:15:13.836
So that's an authentic copy.

00:15:13.836 --> 00:15:15.848
It doesn't have to be identical

00:15:15.848 --> 00:15:19.526
as long as the person
who declares it authentic

00:15:19.526 --> 00:15:22.859
considers it to be essentially the same.

00:15:24.502 --> 00:15:25.988
Then we have the facsimile.

00:15:25.988 --> 00:15:27.950
Anybody can do a facsimile.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:30.449
You can make a facsimile for exhibition.

00:15:30.449 --> 00:15:33.059
What is typical of the facsimile

00:15:33.059 --> 00:15:37.701
is that the medium on which
the document is identical

00:15:37.701 --> 00:15:39.083
to the one of the original.

00:15:39.083 --> 00:15:40.332
It's not just the content.

00:15:40.332 --> 00:15:42.505
The whole thing is identical.

00:15:42.505 --> 00:15:45.422
If you were not actually announcing

00:15:47.149 --> 00:15:48.886
that this was not the original,

00:15:48.886 --> 00:15:53.082
anybody could think
that it is the original.

00:15:53.082 --> 00:15:55.713
Then there is the copy in
the form of the original.

00:15:55.713 --> 00:15:57.144
The copy in the form of the original

00:15:57.144 --> 00:15:58.894
is a second original.

00:15:59.736 --> 00:16:02.762
That is the same person
who has issued the original

00:16:02.762 --> 00:16:04.845
later on issues it again.

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:10.190
So, that means that it is not the first.

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:12.434
Primitiveness is missing.

00:16:12.434 --> 00:16:14.649
But the other two qualities are there,

00:16:14.649 --> 00:16:16.365
which are completeness,

00:16:16.365 --> 00:16:19.462
because it's absolutely
identical to the original,

00:16:19.462 --> 00:16:20.962
and effectiveness.

00:16:21.832 --> 00:16:25.999
An imitative copy is a copy
that looks like the original

00:16:27.223 --> 00:16:31.422
but really it has elements
that make you distinguish it

00:16:31.422 --> 00:16:32.772
from the original.

00:16:32.772 --> 00:16:33.666
A photocopy.

00:16:33.666 --> 00:16:37.384
A photocopy is the typical imitative copy.

00:16:37.384 --> 00:16:40.034
A simple copy is just a transcription.

00:16:40.034 --> 00:16:43.946
It's when you just sit
there and copy document.

00:16:43.946 --> 00:16:45.779
An insert is the copy,

00:16:48.971 --> 00:16:53.406
the transcription of a document
within another document.

00:16:53.406 --> 00:16:57.724
For example, when you
forward an email to somebody,

00:16:57.724 --> 00:17:00.537
what is inside is an insert.

00:17:00.537 --> 00:17:05.036
So, it's a document contained
within another document.

00:17:05.036 --> 00:17:08.953
An inspeximus instead
has really the same force

00:17:10.399 --> 00:17:13.349
as the original and
substitutes the original.

00:17:13.349 --> 00:17:14.266
What is it?

00:17:15.590 --> 00:17:18.423
This is very typical of old times.

00:17:20.563 --> 00:17:23.396
Traditionally in old jurisdictions

00:17:24.845 --> 00:17:29.327
for certain legal acts
the record is the act.

00:17:29.327 --> 00:17:30.910
No record no act.

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:33.660
So, a contract of land ownership.

00:17:36.722 --> 00:17:38.287
You need the record.

00:17:38.287 --> 00:17:40.570
You lose the record,
you don't have the land.

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:43.061
Unless you have a system of registry

00:17:43.061 --> 00:17:45.366
but that didn't exist in the past.

00:17:45.366 --> 00:17:49.287
Or the emperor or the
king gives you a benefit,

00:17:49.287 --> 00:17:51.482
the record is the benefit.

00:17:51.482 --> 00:17:54.143
That gets in a fire, it's gone.

00:17:54.143 --> 00:17:57.148
Or over time they just crumble,

00:17:57.148 --> 00:18:01.347
and as they crumble the
owner would go to a notary,

00:18:01.347 --> 00:18:05.514
a notary public, or a lawyer
and ask for an inspeximus.

00:18:06.711 --> 00:18:11.047
So, the notary public or the
lawyer looks at the document

00:18:11.047 --> 00:18:15.130
and transcribes it writing
I have seen, vidimus,

00:18:16.237 --> 00:18:20.404
or I have inspected, inspeximus,
the original document

00:18:21.598 --> 00:18:24.015
that says so, so, so, and so.

00:18:24.978 --> 00:18:27.860
Transcribes completely, signs it,

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:29.853
you can destroy the original.

00:18:29.853 --> 00:18:34.020
That is the most traditional
way through which the titles

00:18:35.022 --> 00:18:38.463
of old families and cities, et cetera,

00:18:38.463 --> 00:18:40.963
have been preserved over time.

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:44.468
Then the other thing
that you have to look at

00:18:44.468 --> 00:18:48.388
when you look at copy, in
addition to who issues it

00:18:48.388 --> 00:18:51.518
and how much authority it has,

00:18:51.518 --> 00:18:54.185
is also the hierarchy of copies.

00:18:55.604 --> 00:18:57.735
So, you can copy from an original,

00:18:57.735 --> 00:18:59.719
you can copy from the first copy,

00:18:59.719 --> 00:19:02.218
you can copy from the 10th copy.

00:19:02.218 --> 00:19:05.468
The possibility of error keeps growing.

00:19:10.022 --> 00:19:12.519
The monasteries used to copy the text

00:19:12.519 --> 00:19:16.470
in order to make them go through centuries

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:20.451
because, of course, paper
and parchment would crumble

00:19:20.451 --> 00:19:21.956
and the ink would disappear.

00:19:21.956 --> 00:19:23.520
So that was the purpose.

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:27.186
But human error keeps
compounding itself over time.

00:19:27.186 --> 00:19:30.434
So, if you have the second
copy that's more authority

00:19:30.434 --> 00:19:33.333
than if you have the
10th copy of something.

00:19:33.333 --> 00:19:37.500
So that is also one thing
that has to be looked at.

00:19:39.623 --> 00:19:42.023
Now, in the digital
environment, let's move on,

00:19:42.023 --> 00:19:43.289
those are the concepts

00:19:43.289 --> 00:19:45.393
that are at the foundation of everything.

00:19:45.393 --> 00:19:49.630
Now we have to confront
what we are dealing with.

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:51.782
We have digitized originals

00:19:51.782 --> 00:19:55.398
which as originals were on other media

00:19:55.398 --> 00:19:56.854
and we have no problem with them.

00:19:56.854 --> 00:19:59.887
They correspond pretty
much to an authentic copy.

00:19:59.887 --> 00:20:02.130
You digitize it and you declare

00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:05.458
that it is conformed to the original.

00:20:05.458 --> 00:20:06.791
All the aspects,

00:20:09.249 --> 00:20:11.895
that is everything but the medium.

00:20:11.895 --> 00:20:15.778
But all the rest is as
if it were the original.

00:20:15.778 --> 00:20:17.921
So there is no problem there.

00:20:17.921 --> 00:20:20.226
Born digital originals, we already said,

00:20:20.226 --> 00:20:22.005
exist for a nano-second.

00:20:22.005 --> 00:20:23.506
So what are we dealing with?

00:20:23.506 --> 00:20:26.109
We deal with synchronic copies

00:20:26.109 --> 00:20:29.774
that is copies made all at the same time,

00:20:29.774 --> 00:20:33.857
for example, when you send
a message to 20 people

00:20:34.703 --> 00:20:36.187
at the same time.

00:20:36.187 --> 00:20:38.796
We're dealing with diachronic copies,

00:20:38.796 --> 00:20:42.616
for example, when you saved
them to an external drive,

00:20:42.616 --> 00:20:46.783
or when you make after the time
you regularly copy something

00:20:47.736 --> 00:20:50.653
for redundancy so that you are sure

00:20:51.627 --> 00:20:54.743
that if something gets
destroyed you still have an item

00:20:54.743 --> 00:20:55.913
somewhere.

00:20:55.913 --> 00:20:59.735
Or for distribution to
a variety of repository,

00:20:59.735 --> 00:21:01.366
the log system.

00:21:01.366 --> 00:21:03.949
Lots of copies make stuff safe.

00:21:05.012 --> 00:21:06.845
That's the log system.

00:21:07.748 --> 00:21:11.913
One thing we have learned
is that all the copies

00:21:11.913 --> 00:21:14.798
made through redundancy and distribution

00:21:14.798 --> 00:21:17.948
are never identical to each other.

00:21:17.948 --> 00:21:21.523
If anything, at least the
metadata related to time

00:21:21.523 --> 00:21:22.440
is changed.

00:21:23.573 --> 00:21:25.660
Never identical.

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:26.826
The other thing we learned

00:21:26.826 --> 00:21:30.685
is you cannot preserve digital documents.

00:21:30.685 --> 00:21:35.229
You can only preserve the
ability to re-produce them

00:21:35.229 --> 00:21:37.098
or to re-create them,

00:21:37.098 --> 00:21:39.922
and we will see the difference later on.

00:21:39.922 --> 00:21:43.641
However, in order to re-produce
or re-create a document

00:21:43.641 --> 00:21:46.483
you have to have a document
in the first place,

00:21:46.483 --> 00:21:50.650
that is you need to have
fixed form and stable content.

00:21:52.011 --> 00:21:53.698
What is a document with fixed form

00:21:53.698 --> 00:21:55.798
in the digital environment?

00:21:55.798 --> 00:21:59.746
Okay, this is a type,
it's a digital entity

00:21:59.746 --> 00:22:03.691
whose binary content
is stored in such a way

00:22:03.691 --> 00:22:07.961
that what you see on the
screen every time you recall it

00:22:07.961 --> 00:22:11.715
is identical to what you have seen before.

00:22:11.715 --> 00:22:16.108
That is, the documentary
presentation remains constant,

00:22:16.108 --> 00:22:20.275
even if the technological or
digital presentation changes.

00:22:22.630 --> 00:22:26.795
So, you can move a
document from Word to PDF.

00:22:26.795 --> 00:22:29.995
It's a different digital format.

00:22:29.995 --> 00:22:32.574
But what you see on
the screen is the same.

00:22:32.574 --> 00:22:36.089
So that is a document with fixed form.

00:22:36.089 --> 00:22:40.103
But also a document has fixed
form if the same content

00:22:40.103 --> 00:22:44.805
can be presented on the screen
in several different ways

00:22:44.805 --> 00:22:48.222
but in a limited number of possibilities.

00:22:50.703 --> 00:22:54.870
The same stored entity can
show up in different forms.

00:22:56.894 --> 00:23:00.326
For example, statistical data
can be viewed as a pie chart,

00:23:00.326 --> 00:23:02.614
a bar chart, or a table.

00:23:02.614 --> 00:23:04.533
But it is a limited number

00:23:04.533 --> 00:23:09.348
and it is always the same
possibilities and the same data.

00:23:09.348 --> 00:23:11.015
Stable content means

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:14.599
that the data and the message it conveys

00:23:14.599 --> 00:23:17.719
are unchanged and unchangeable.

00:23:17.719 --> 00:23:21.266
So the data cannot be
overwritten, altered,

00:23:21.266 --> 00:23:22.933
deleted or added to.

00:23:24.355 --> 00:23:28.522
Or it means that the changes
are limited and controlled

00:23:30.551 --> 00:23:32.336
by fixed rules

00:23:32.336 --> 00:23:36.736
so that the same query
generates the same outcome,

00:23:36.736 --> 00:23:40.710
and we have different views of
different subsets of content.

00:23:40.710 --> 00:23:44.286
For example, a library
catalog, an archival inventory.

00:23:44.286 --> 00:23:46.244
You never see it altogether, right?

00:23:46.244 --> 00:23:49.962
You do a query and you see
the entry that you want.

00:23:49.962 --> 00:23:54.129
But the same query always will
get you the same response.

00:23:56.666 --> 00:24:00.249
So that is still
considered stable content.

00:24:02.272 --> 00:24:05.980
When we think about a document
in the digital environment

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:10.465
in terms of fixed and stability
and authenticity, et cetera,

00:24:10.465 --> 00:24:13.773
we have to think of three
parts, not just one.

00:24:13.773 --> 00:24:16.127
It's not just this item that you pick up,

00:24:16.127 --> 00:24:19.502
it's three things that
live quite separately

00:24:19.502 --> 00:24:21.081
from each other.

00:24:21.081 --> 00:24:23.561
One is the formal
elements of the document,

00:24:23.561 --> 00:24:25.355
which are the traditional ones

00:24:25.355 --> 00:24:28.350
we see on the face of a document:

00:24:28.350 --> 00:24:32.433
the date, the address,
the salutation, et cetera.

00:24:33.442 --> 00:24:35.912
The other one is the metadata,

00:24:35.912 --> 00:24:38.542
which are the attributes of the document.

00:24:38.542 --> 00:24:41.174
Now, the metadata are not an invention

00:24:41.174 --> 00:24:43.156
of the digital environment.

00:24:43.156 --> 00:24:45.573
We didn't call that metadata.

00:24:46.973 --> 00:24:51.556
We call this intrinsic
elements of the document.

00:24:51.556 --> 00:24:54.389
Think of some old document folded,

00:24:56.084 --> 00:24:59.001
you fold it and put it on the back,

00:25:00.291 --> 00:25:04.041
on the dorsum, that is
an endorsement, right?

00:25:05.941 --> 00:25:08.421
Which has the date of the document,

00:25:08.421 --> 00:25:10.615
the name of the addressee,

00:25:10.615 --> 00:25:13.082
the name of who is keeping the document,

00:25:13.082 --> 00:25:15.170
the subject of the document.

00:25:15.170 --> 00:25:17.186
Those are the metadata.

00:25:17.186 --> 00:25:19.094
On the front of the document

00:25:19.094 --> 00:25:21.383
what do we write when we receive it?

00:25:21.383 --> 00:25:23.050
The date of receipt.

00:25:24.358 --> 00:25:25.691
What do we write

00:25:26.848 --> 00:25:30.087
or stamp with the name of
the office receiving it.

00:25:30.087 --> 00:25:31.948
Those are all the metadata.

00:25:31.948 --> 00:25:35.471
The same metadata are
needed in our present

00:25:35.471 --> 00:25:37.422
in the digital records

00:25:37.422 --> 00:25:40.887
except that they are
transparent to the user,

00:25:40.887 --> 00:25:42.867
you don't see them.

00:25:42.867 --> 00:25:45.243
On the paper you do.

00:25:45.243 --> 00:25:46.980
Digital components.

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:51.075
Digital components are
those stored entities

00:25:51.075 --> 00:25:54.992
that are what you actually
preserve or conserve

00:25:56.378 --> 00:25:58.128
in the digital world.

00:25:59.194 --> 00:26:02.737
You don't see them and
they do not correspond

00:26:02.737 --> 00:26:05.045
to the part of the document.

00:26:05.045 --> 00:26:08.828
So for example, if you have 10 memos

00:26:08.828 --> 00:26:10.783
that are all in the same format

00:26:10.783 --> 00:26:12.499
and they're all textual,

00:26:12.499 --> 00:26:16.666
they can all be in one
component, one digital component.

00:26:17.998 --> 00:26:20.263
But if you have an email

00:26:20.263 --> 00:26:23.067
with attachments and
the digital signature,

00:26:23.067 --> 00:26:27.354
you have the header, the
message, the attachment,

00:26:27.354 --> 00:26:28.454
and the digital signature.

00:26:28.454 --> 00:26:30.178
There're five components

00:26:30.178 --> 00:26:33.246
that go in five different
parts of the system

00:26:33.246 --> 00:26:36.618
and need a different preservation measure.

00:26:36.618 --> 00:26:40.101
So, you understand that
here you're dealing

00:26:40.101 --> 00:26:45.041
at a completely different
level, with different parts

00:26:45.041 --> 00:26:48.457
that eventually to
re-produce the document,

00:26:48.457 --> 00:26:52.624
so that you see on the screen,
have to get altogether.

00:26:55.012 --> 00:26:59.179
Because what we never had
before is the difference

00:27:00.683 --> 00:27:05.482
between a stored document
and a manifested document.

00:27:05.482 --> 00:27:08.701
In fact, with a digital
entity we have at least

00:27:08.701 --> 00:27:11.625
two documents every single time.

00:27:11.625 --> 00:27:14.981
The stored document is
made up of the components

00:27:14.981 --> 00:27:17.742
used in re-producing the document,

00:27:17.742 --> 00:27:20.937
that is in producing it
again when you recall it,

00:27:20.937 --> 00:27:24.515
and they are the data to be processed

00:27:24.515 --> 00:27:28.893
in order to show the document,
which are content data

00:27:28.893 --> 00:27:30.060
and form data.

00:27:31.827 --> 00:27:34.442
The rules for processing them

00:27:34.442 --> 00:27:37.640
which are the composition data.

00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:40.801
That's all the stored document.

00:27:40.801 --> 00:27:44.589
The manifested document
is the visualization

00:27:44.589 --> 00:27:47.664
in a form that is suitable
for reading to a person

00:27:47.664 --> 00:27:48.969
or to a system.

00:27:48.969 --> 00:27:53.662
Now, sometimes you don't
have this manifestation.

00:27:53.662 --> 00:27:57.329
For example, you go to
the ATM to get money.

00:27:58.366 --> 00:27:59.769
You put your card in there

00:27:59.769 --> 00:28:02.059
and something happens within the system,

00:28:02.059 --> 00:28:03.934
that is the system is checking

00:28:03.934 --> 00:28:05.990
whether you have got the money,

00:28:05.990 --> 00:28:08.543
and then decides to give you some,

00:28:08.543 --> 00:28:10.140
and then makes all the calculations,

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:13.866
subtracts your money from what you had.

00:28:13.866 --> 00:28:18.214
Every single action
generates stored records,

00:28:18.214 --> 00:28:22.381
that is content data, form
data, and composition data.

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:26.576
That the system sees
but you will never see.

00:28:26.576 --> 00:28:29.826
What you get is the receipt at the end.

00:28:34.003 --> 00:28:37.253
In other cases you do not have a stored

00:28:38.248 --> 00:28:39.915
manifested document.

00:28:42.006 --> 00:28:44.089
But you can re-create it.

00:28:45.312 --> 00:28:47.713
Like in a relational database.

00:28:47.713 --> 00:28:51.921
So, when you have a relational
database and you ask a query,

00:28:51.921 --> 00:28:53.487
a document shows up.

00:28:53.487 --> 00:28:58.456
You can decide to save it as
such, as what you actually see,

00:28:58.456 --> 00:29:00.881
in which case you have
a manifested document,

00:29:00.881 --> 00:29:02.445
you have a stored document.

00:29:02.445 --> 00:29:04.814
Or you don't save it because you know

00:29:04.814 --> 00:29:06.367
that every time you want to see it

00:29:06.367 --> 00:29:08.561
you just go through the same query.

00:29:08.561 --> 00:29:12.324
So in that case you re-create
the document every time.

00:29:12.324 --> 00:29:16.491
You see you're now re-producing,
you're re-creating it.

00:29:18.935 --> 00:29:21.625
Types of digital documents, two types.

00:29:21.625 --> 00:29:23.901
Static, the ones that only allow you

00:29:23.901 --> 00:29:27.163
to open, close and
navigate and nothing else.

00:29:27.163 --> 00:29:28.644
And interactive.

00:29:28.644 --> 00:29:32.208
They have variable
content, form, et cetera,

00:29:32.208 --> 00:29:34.315
but the rules are fixed.

00:29:34.315 --> 00:29:38.482
That is, whatever you do
always you get the same output.

00:29:40.243 --> 00:29:42.259
So in light of all the above,

00:29:42.259 --> 00:29:44.715
when we talk about digital copies,

00:29:44.715 --> 00:29:48.882
what you need to do is to
identify the authoritative copy

00:29:50.067 --> 00:29:52.081
because we have millions of that.

00:29:52.081 --> 00:29:54.773
And the best way of identifying them,

00:29:54.773 --> 00:29:56.693
there're other ways that I will mention,

00:29:56.693 --> 00:30:00.045
but the best way is to
look at the metadata,

00:30:00.045 --> 00:30:04.954
which are the primary means
to provide authority to a copy

00:30:04.954 --> 00:30:08.204
or to identify the authority of a copy.

00:30:10.976 --> 00:30:12.845
Okay, identity metadata.

00:30:12.845 --> 00:30:14.441
Definition.

00:30:14.441 --> 00:30:18.351
Identity metadata are all
the attributes of a document

00:30:18.351 --> 00:30:21.934
that characterize it
as unique, altogether.

00:30:23.004 --> 00:30:26.815
They make it unique, it's
like your identity card.

00:30:26.815 --> 00:30:30.757
It says where you were
born, when you were born,

00:30:30.757 --> 00:30:33.257
and where you live, et cetera.

00:30:34.195 --> 00:30:37.717
So all those elements,
they make up your card.

00:30:37.717 --> 00:30:39.502
This is the same thing.

00:30:39.502 --> 00:30:43.669
Identity metadata are unique
together for each document.

00:30:45.292 --> 00:30:48.604
And these are the ones that
we call identity metadata:

00:30:48.604 --> 00:30:50.505
the names of the persons that concur

00:30:50.505 --> 00:30:53.715
to the creation of that document;

00:30:53.715 --> 00:30:57.698
the dates, times of genesis,
issuing and transmission;

00:30:57.698 --> 00:31:02.136
matter or subject;
relationships; form name;

00:31:02.136 --> 00:31:04.971
format presentation; attachments;

00:31:04.971 --> 00:31:07.328
and if applicable a digital signature.

00:31:07.328 --> 00:31:09.369
You actually don't need anything more.

00:31:09.369 --> 00:31:11.778
In fact we were saying today at lunch

00:31:11.778 --> 00:31:14.344
that really all you need is the expression

00:31:14.344 --> 00:31:17.027
of the relationship to other documents.

00:31:17.027 --> 00:31:19.243
Because once you identify that,

00:31:19.243 --> 00:31:21.498
then you can go to all the rest,

00:31:21.498 --> 00:31:23.582
you can access everything else.

00:31:23.582 --> 00:31:26.332
You have the classification code,

00:31:27.248 --> 00:31:29.693
like a person has an ID, that's it.

00:31:29.693 --> 00:31:32.693
That's the person, from
there I can go anywhere.

00:31:32.693 --> 00:31:36.860
So, this idea of having metadata
for under 25 attributes,

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:42.477
it's really not only
non-realistic but not needed.

00:31:44.779 --> 00:31:45.769
Integrity.

00:31:45.769 --> 00:31:47.931
A document has integrity if the message

00:31:47.931 --> 00:31:52.250
it is meant to communicate
to achieve its purpose

00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:53.367
has not changed.

00:31:53.367 --> 00:31:54.200
You see,

00:31:55.233 --> 00:31:59.400
the message needed to
communicate has not changed.

00:32:01.236 --> 00:32:04.034
Okay, so we have traditionally said

00:32:04.034 --> 00:32:07.867
we infer integrity from
the fact that we trust

00:32:10.013 --> 00:32:12.372
whoever is keeping the record.

00:32:12.372 --> 00:32:16.039
So, you deduce integrity
from responsibility

00:32:18.236 --> 00:32:20.915
and you say, if I know
the names of the person

00:32:20.915 --> 00:32:23.437
that handled the document over time,

00:32:23.437 --> 00:32:26.758
if I know he's responsible
for keeping the record,

00:32:26.758 --> 00:32:30.029
if there are annotations
related to changes,

00:32:30.029 --> 00:32:33.535
but technical changes and any other kind,

00:32:33.535 --> 00:32:36.542
if there is a digital
signature, et cetera,

00:32:36.542 --> 00:32:39.734
if I have these elements, then I can say,

00:32:39.734 --> 00:32:41.567
I can infer integrity.

00:32:43.571 --> 00:32:46.944
Which is the quality of
being complete and unaltered

00:32:46.944 --> 00:32:49.676
in all essential respects.

00:32:49.676 --> 00:32:52.999
And we have never been fussy about it.

00:32:52.999 --> 00:32:56.758
So, if you had a document
with a hole in the middle,

00:32:56.758 --> 00:33:01.115
or yellowish, or with
the ink fading aways,

00:33:01.115 --> 00:33:03.910
or with the corners
burnt, you wouldn't say,

00:33:03.910 --> 00:33:06.345
"Oh, it has lost integrity
so we don't want it.

00:33:06.345 --> 00:33:08.103
"It's no longer authentic."

00:33:08.103 --> 00:33:10.043
You would say it's good enough, right?

00:33:10.043 --> 00:33:12.460
As long as I know what it is.

00:33:13.386 --> 00:33:17.787
Well, is that different in
the digital environment?

00:33:17.787 --> 00:33:18.965
Let's see.

00:33:18.965 --> 00:33:23.098
Loss of integrity of an analog document.

00:33:23.098 --> 00:33:24.519
Can still read it.

00:33:24.519 --> 00:33:25.602
Still manage.

00:33:26.633 --> 00:33:29.449
Still okay, you still
have the same document.

00:33:29.449 --> 00:33:31.338
Now, a digital document.

00:33:31.338 --> 00:33:34.921
If the original bits
are 101, it is a five.

00:33:35.755 --> 00:33:37.922
If it is 110, it is a six.

00:33:39.745 --> 00:33:42.078
If it is 011, it is a three.

00:33:44.329 --> 00:33:46.983
Same bits, different value.

00:33:46.983 --> 00:33:47.816
Why?

00:33:47.816 --> 00:33:49.197
Because the order counts.

00:33:49.197 --> 00:33:50.864
Not just the number.

00:33:51.947 --> 00:33:55.708
So, you can see that we are
not talking about fading away,

00:33:55.708 --> 00:33:59.277
we are talking about losing
bits we lose the record.

00:33:59.277 --> 00:34:02.175
Because we are talking
about data integrity

00:34:02.175 --> 00:34:05.194
based on bitwise integrity,

00:34:05.194 --> 00:34:07.885
the fact that the data are
not modified intentionally

00:34:07.885 --> 00:34:09.643
or accidentally.

00:34:09.643 --> 00:34:13.097
The bits are in a complete
and unaltered state.

00:34:13.097 --> 00:34:16.681
So, a small change in the
bits means a different value

00:34:16.681 --> 00:34:20.213
on the screen and a different action.

00:34:20.213 --> 00:34:22.351
How do we protect them?

00:34:22.351 --> 00:34:24.964
Well, intentional
alteration is preventable

00:34:24.964 --> 00:34:28.565
through permission and access
control, access privileges,

00:34:28.565 --> 00:34:31.351
or through Checksum and HASH Algorithms.

00:34:31.351 --> 00:34:34.125
Accidental alteration is preventable

00:34:34.125 --> 00:34:36.909
through special hardware and software.

00:34:36.909 --> 00:34:39.612
But the problem is that we
have to be able to determine

00:34:39.612 --> 00:34:40.808
that alteration has happened,

00:34:40.808 --> 00:34:44.265
most of the times we don't really know.

00:34:44.265 --> 00:34:46.377
And so, what do we do?

00:34:46.377 --> 00:34:48.054
Do we go and check the logs?

00:34:48.054 --> 00:34:50.740
Yes, on a regular way we could.

00:34:50.740 --> 00:34:53.617
But in fact, we have to infer integrity

00:34:53.617 --> 00:34:56.292
till such time when it is questioned,

00:34:56.292 --> 00:34:59.042
and then you have the logs to control it.

00:34:59.042 --> 00:35:01.615
Otherwise, there is no other way.

00:35:01.615 --> 00:35:06.006
And as regards copies, because
we live in a copy world,

00:35:06.006 --> 00:35:10.230
in a world of copies, duplication
integrity is important.

00:35:10.230 --> 00:35:11.966
It's the process.

00:35:11.966 --> 00:35:15.105
So given a document, a digital document,

00:35:15.105 --> 00:35:17.949
you must be sure that
the process of copying it

00:35:17.949 --> 00:35:19.449
doesn't change it.

00:35:22.014 --> 00:35:25.014
Often this change is linked to time,

00:35:26.391 --> 00:35:30.536
that is, today wouldn't
but tomorrow it would,

00:35:30.536 --> 00:35:32.396
the process that you carry out.

00:35:32.396 --> 00:35:36.479
Because it depends on what
else is in the system.

00:35:38.266 --> 00:35:41.505
And of course we do
duplication all the time,

00:35:41.505 --> 00:35:44.753
but not necessarily
the output is the same.

00:35:44.753 --> 00:35:46.920
When we talk about a copy,

00:35:48.264 --> 00:35:51.960
we talk about a selective duplicate.

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:56.440
A selective duplicate is
never the same as the original

00:35:56.440 --> 00:35:59.992
because you can only
copy what you can see,

00:35:59.992 --> 00:36:01.999
and on paper it was very easy

00:36:01.999 --> 00:36:04.266
because you could see the metadata.

00:36:04.266 --> 00:36:09.105
You can't see the metadata
in the digital environment,

00:36:09.105 --> 00:36:11.938
or other things like the metadata.

00:36:12.848 --> 00:36:15.876
There is rarely
confirmation of completeness

00:36:15.876 --> 00:36:19.066
and there is an incomplete
picture of the environment.

00:36:19.066 --> 00:36:21.341
The image is a forensic duplication,

00:36:21.341 --> 00:36:25.248
is a bit by bit
reproduction of everything.

00:36:25.248 --> 00:36:28.797
But you may not want to have
that sort of duplication

00:36:28.797 --> 00:36:29.915
to deal with

00:36:29.915 --> 00:36:31.935
because then you would
have to maintain it,

00:36:31.935 --> 00:36:34.850
all those parts like
swap space, slack space,

00:36:34.850 --> 00:36:37.523
ambient data, et cetera, that are useless.

00:36:37.523 --> 00:36:39.023
You don't want to.

00:36:40.001 --> 00:36:44.431
So, how do you have an
authentic duplication?

00:36:44.431 --> 00:36:48.729
You have an authentic
duplication when in some way

00:36:48.729 --> 00:36:51.704
you can maintain a native format.

00:36:51.704 --> 00:36:54.954
Now, over time we have been used to PDF

00:36:56.755 --> 00:36:59.985
for delivering page-based documents.

00:36:59.985 --> 00:37:04.115
We use for preservation PDF/A

00:37:04.115 --> 00:37:07.782
because it allows to
maintain all the fonts.

00:37:09.765 --> 00:37:14.113
PDF/A-2 increases accessibility,

00:37:14.113 --> 00:37:18.086
but PDF/A-3 is where we're heading now.

00:37:18.086 --> 00:37:21.290
Because what it does is to provide you

00:37:21.290 --> 00:37:25.457
the service of PDF/A-1, that
is, you see the document

00:37:29.758 --> 00:37:33.008
as you chose permanently on the screen,

00:37:34.084 --> 00:37:37.584
but it can embed all the previous formats.

00:37:38.995 --> 00:37:42.463
So, it is what is used
now to address the issues

00:37:42.463 --> 00:37:46.546
of obsolescence and of
readability and usability.

00:37:48.249 --> 00:37:52.014
It gives you the main display document

00:37:52.014 --> 00:37:54.849
in a stable and fixed way,

00:37:54.849 --> 00:37:59.016
and the metadata set can be
stored in the dumb data sections

00:38:00.609 --> 00:38:02.744
to ensure authenticity.

00:38:02.744 --> 00:38:06.125
So, any concern about best
evidence and data integrity

00:38:06.125 --> 00:38:10.452
can be addressed, embedding
the bit-stream of the source

00:38:10.452 --> 00:38:12.619
within this document.

00:38:12.619 --> 00:38:15.952
So, this is what now is considered to be

00:38:16.906 --> 00:38:21.073
the copy that is appropriate
mostly against obsolescence

00:38:23.579 --> 00:38:27.746
and for the purposes of best
evidence in a court of law.

00:38:28.782 --> 00:38:31.729
The process through which we do it

00:38:31.729 --> 00:38:36.013
must follow the principle
of non-interference,

00:38:36.013 --> 00:38:38.613
which means that the method you use

00:38:38.613 --> 00:38:40.878
is not changing anything.

00:38:40.878 --> 00:38:44.851
Or the principle of
identifiable interference:

00:38:44.851 --> 00:38:48.650
if it is changing anything
you say what it is,

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:50.317
you can identify it.

00:38:53.012 --> 00:38:54.288
I don't think we have the time

00:38:54.288 --> 00:38:56.955
to talk about digital signature.

00:38:58.290 --> 00:39:01.457
I have five minutes, we have the time.

00:39:03.496 --> 00:39:06.223
There is a difference between authenticity

00:39:06.223 --> 00:39:09.352
and authentication that is key.

00:39:09.352 --> 00:39:12.472
Because authenticity is a
quality of the document,

00:39:12.472 --> 00:39:15.387
either it's authentic or it is.

00:39:15.387 --> 00:39:17.699
Authentication is a
declaration that the document

00:39:17.699 --> 00:39:20.514
is authentic in that specific moment,

00:39:20.514 --> 00:39:23.659
but not necessarily the next day.

00:39:23.659 --> 00:39:27.310
So, today we use digital
signature for authentication

00:39:27.310 --> 00:39:31.945
and digital signature is not
a signature, it is a seal.

00:39:31.945 --> 00:39:36.758
Because it is an attachment
to a complete document

00:39:36.758 --> 00:39:38.458
that already has its own signature

00:39:38.458 --> 00:39:39.509
or whatever it needs to have.

00:39:39.509 --> 00:39:41.496
It's complete as is.

00:39:41.496 --> 00:39:43.770
It verifies the origin of the document,

00:39:43.770 --> 00:39:47.541
certifies the intactness of the document,

00:39:47.541 --> 00:39:51.653
it makes authorship and
ownership indisputable,

00:39:51.653 --> 00:39:53.736
that is, non-repudiation.

00:39:56.504 --> 00:40:00.898
The digital signature,
talking about copies,

00:40:00.898 --> 00:40:05.266
is said to provide
incontrovertible mechanism

00:40:05.266 --> 00:40:08.260
for verifying the authenticity
of digital objects.

00:40:08.260 --> 00:40:09.427
Right, accept.

00:40:10.769 --> 00:40:12.534
What it is that it verifies?

00:40:12.534 --> 00:40:15.368
That the person who claims
to have sent the document

00:40:15.368 --> 00:40:19.368
is in fact the person who
claims, who is, right?

00:40:20.559 --> 00:40:22.768
And it verifies that the document

00:40:22.768 --> 00:40:25.465
that was put into the system and sent

00:40:25.465 --> 00:40:27.231
has not been altered.

00:40:27.231 --> 00:40:30.532
It does not verify that
the copy is actually

00:40:30.532 --> 00:40:33.835
a copy of the document
that it claims to be.

00:40:33.835 --> 00:40:35.970
So, we're back to the same thing.

00:40:35.970 --> 00:40:39.906
All this mechanical
system, they just prove you

00:40:39.906 --> 00:40:44.073
that what you receive is
what was sent by the person.

00:40:44.953 --> 00:40:47.738
But that person is sending
you the copy of the document

00:40:47.738 --> 00:40:51.288
that she or he is claiming to be something

00:40:51.288 --> 00:40:52.256
that you can't prove,

00:40:52.256 --> 00:40:56.481
certainly not with the digital signature.

00:40:56.481 --> 00:40:58.965
Besides, digital signatures are good

00:40:58.965 --> 00:41:01.673
for ensuring authenticity of documents

00:41:01.673 --> 00:41:05.541
as they move across space
from a system to another.

00:41:05.541 --> 00:41:06.739
Not across time.

00:41:06.739 --> 00:41:09.735
Because it becomes obsolete
before the document

00:41:09.735 --> 00:41:13.902
to which it is attached and
cannot really be preserved.

00:41:14.836 --> 00:41:17.953
And memory institutions
are not maintaining them.

00:41:17.953 --> 00:41:20.362
So, what's better to use?

00:41:20.362 --> 00:41:22.533
A chain of legitimate custody

00:41:22.533 --> 00:41:25.421
is ground for inferring authenticity.

00:41:25.421 --> 00:41:28.252
The digital chain of
custody is the least of all

00:41:28.252 --> 00:41:31.090
that has been done to the
record for the document

00:41:31.090 --> 00:41:32.987
from beginning to end.

00:41:32.987 --> 00:41:36.253
And a declaration on the
trustworthiness of the system

00:41:36.253 --> 00:41:38.983
reading which the document exists.

00:41:38.983 --> 00:41:41.965
So especially the court's now saying,

00:41:41.965 --> 00:41:45.483
"If I can trust that nobody
has interfered with the system,

00:41:45.483 --> 00:41:48.908
"so I will believe that the document

00:41:48.908 --> 00:41:50.676
"and the system are okay."

00:41:50.676 --> 00:41:52.940
It's an inference again.

00:41:52.940 --> 00:41:55.353
So, we get to the main slide here.

00:41:55.353 --> 00:41:57.337
Involuntary secondary permanence.

00:41:57.337 --> 00:41:58.844
Where?

00:41:58.844 --> 00:42:01.209
Now, by now you know

00:42:01.209 --> 00:42:03.863
we have already lost track of everything.

00:42:03.863 --> 00:42:05.954
The copies are uncontrollable.

00:42:05.954 --> 00:42:07.334
They're everywhere.

00:42:07.334 --> 00:42:09.356
They're distributed, they're redundant,

00:42:09.356 --> 00:42:12.172
there're lots of authenticated copies

00:42:12.172 --> 00:42:14.732
for preservation, et cetera.

00:42:14.732 --> 00:42:16.462
And we don't really want to keep them,

00:42:16.462 --> 00:42:17.803
but we don't know where to find them

00:42:17.803 --> 00:42:20.537
in order to destroy them so we give up.

00:42:20.537 --> 00:42:21.851
That's the situation.

00:42:21.851 --> 00:42:24.483
Except it's not much of a problem.

00:42:24.483 --> 00:42:27.093
If we have an in-house system,

00:42:27.093 --> 00:42:31.665
if everything is inside our
building and our servers,

00:42:31.665 --> 00:42:35.832
it clogs the system, but
really is not such a problem.

00:42:36.857 --> 00:42:40.291
What if the documents are not with us?

00:42:40.291 --> 00:42:42.829
What if the documents are in the custody

00:42:42.829 --> 00:42:44.746
of some cloud provider,

00:42:46.038 --> 00:42:48.081
of some other third party?

00:42:48.081 --> 00:42:51.527
How do we prove the
trustworthiness of the copies

00:42:51.527 --> 00:42:54.398
that they have and they control?

00:42:54.398 --> 00:42:58.240
That's why we created the
fourth phase of InterPARES,

00:42:58.240 --> 00:43:00.490
InterPARES Trust or Itrust.

00:43:03.232 --> 00:43:07.189
This is the fourth phase
of the research project.

00:43:07.189 --> 00:43:09.241
Its goal is to generate

00:43:09.241 --> 00:43:12.239
theoretical and methodological frameworks

00:43:12.239 --> 00:43:14.677
to support the development of networks

00:43:14.677 --> 00:43:17.237
of policies, procedures,
regulations, standards

00:43:17.237 --> 00:43:20.255
and legislation concerning
digital documents

00:43:20.255 --> 00:43:22.422
entrusted to the Internet.

00:43:23.749 --> 00:43:26.462
It is funded for five years.

00:43:26.462 --> 00:43:31.063
It includes about 300
partners from six continents

00:43:31.063 --> 00:43:32.730
and 35 countries,

00:43:32.730 --> 00:43:36.875
and this is in order to ensure
that whatever we generate

00:43:36.875 --> 00:43:38.625
is consistent across.

00:43:40.277 --> 00:43:42.756
The reason is that the
documentary memory of the world

00:43:42.756 --> 00:43:44.848
is increasingly in the cloud

00:43:44.848 --> 00:43:48.238
because archives and libraries
have to acquire material

00:43:48.238 --> 00:43:51.219
that already exists in the cloud.

00:43:51.219 --> 00:43:55.386
They have to provide access
from everywhere to anybody.

00:43:57.526 --> 00:44:02.020
The systems that are required
by ISO are not affordable.

00:44:02.020 --> 00:44:05.227
The knowledge required to
deal with this material

00:44:05.227 --> 00:44:06.727
is not affordable.

00:44:08.512 --> 00:44:11.911
Many believe that protection
equals preservation,

00:44:11.911 --> 00:44:15.411
which is not true, but under this illusion

00:44:16.258 --> 00:44:17.458
this is what they do.

00:44:17.458 --> 00:44:21.540
But mostly we are confronted
with a generational change

00:44:21.540 --> 00:44:24.540
where the younger people, post-1981,

00:44:26.125 --> 00:44:30.931
they integrate public and
private, they co-author things,

00:44:30.931 --> 00:44:34.870
there is crowdsourcing,
there is co-owning, sharing,

00:44:34.870 --> 00:44:39.037
working from home, bringing
your own device to work,

00:44:40.223 --> 00:44:43.311
and the material is on
a variety of clouds.

00:44:43.311 --> 00:44:45.020
Convergence of media.

00:44:45.020 --> 00:44:47.558
If I tell you that I will
meet you at 5 o'clock

00:44:47.558 --> 00:44:50.323
I send you a Google map, right?

00:44:50.323 --> 00:44:52.656
I don't give you an address.

00:44:53.525 --> 00:44:57.192
Constant connectivity,
liquid communication,

00:44:58.064 --> 00:45:02.338
and enormous amount of
ephemeral material generated.

00:45:02.338 --> 00:45:04.321
What is its characteristics?

00:45:04.321 --> 00:45:06.780
They can be viewed differently

00:45:06.780 --> 00:45:11.502
based on choice of browser,
application, user preferences.

00:45:11.502 --> 00:45:14.001
So it is impossible to say which one

00:45:14.001 --> 00:45:16.418
is the authoritative version.

00:45:17.824 --> 00:45:19.632
Unless you have the authoritative version

00:45:19.632 --> 00:45:22.453
in your in-house system.

00:45:22.453 --> 00:45:25.097
Metadata may be constructed by anybody

00:45:25.097 --> 00:45:28.206
to manipulate the behavior of the system,

00:45:28.206 --> 00:45:30.319
not just to describe it.

00:45:30.319 --> 00:45:34.890
If the goal is communication,
you have a specific way

00:45:34.890 --> 00:45:38.597
of having the document to self-destruct.

00:45:38.597 --> 00:45:42.571
But if you don't do that nobody
is going to destroy them.

00:45:42.571 --> 00:45:47.386
When Google promised to eliminate
with international court,

00:45:47.386 --> 00:45:51.449
you know the verdict to
eliminate the documents

00:45:51.449 --> 00:45:54.120
related to a specific situation,

00:45:54.120 --> 00:45:56.718
he cut the links, he didn't
eliminate the documents,

00:45:56.718 --> 00:45:57.904
so documents are there.

00:45:57.904 --> 00:46:00.186
So, if you use a different search

00:46:00.186 --> 00:46:01.982
rather than the name of the person

00:46:01.982 --> 00:46:05.782
your search copyright in a
specific kind of articles,

00:46:05.782 --> 00:46:07.727
you'll find all articles again.

00:46:07.727 --> 00:46:11.644
So, everything is there,
it will never go away.

00:46:12.649 --> 00:46:15.576
So never put up there anything
you don't want to be used

00:46:15.576 --> 00:46:20.234
against your grandchildren
in the next 100 years.

00:46:20.234 --> 00:46:21.722
If the goal is memory,

00:46:21.722 --> 00:46:24.275
now people create their
own digital archives

00:46:24.275 --> 00:46:27.525
out of documents taken from everywhere.

00:46:29.521 --> 00:46:33.129
The primary challenges,
very quickly, are the reuse,

00:46:33.129 --> 00:46:36.299
the creation of derivative works.

00:46:36.299 --> 00:46:40.597
The sharing, movement of material
from one circle of people

00:46:40.597 --> 00:46:44.206
to another, constantly numerable copies;

00:46:44.206 --> 00:46:46.997
employees collectively create bodies

00:46:46.997 --> 00:46:48.577
of interlinked material.

00:46:48.577 --> 00:46:52.394
Government of Canada
Wikipedia: 150 departments,

00:46:52.394 --> 00:46:56.477
250 thousand employees
create documents together.

00:46:59.379 --> 00:47:04.315
And so, nobody knows to which
agency each document belongs.

00:47:04.315 --> 00:47:08.208
And when it's been completed or generated.

00:47:08.208 --> 00:47:10.317
Location independence.

00:47:10.317 --> 00:47:14.069
The jurisdiction of the entity
responsible for preservation

00:47:14.069 --> 00:47:16.222
is different from the
jurisdiction of the entity

00:47:16.222 --> 00:47:18.222
responsible for storage.

00:47:19.302 --> 00:47:22.643
And so, involuntary permanence is rising

00:47:22.643 --> 00:47:24.560
to a whole other level.

00:47:27.052 --> 00:47:29.967
You know what the cloud is
so we don't need to say.

00:47:29.967 --> 00:47:33.736
The issues obviously are: transparency;

00:47:33.736 --> 00:47:36.570
ownership, authorship, creatorship;

00:47:36.570 --> 00:47:41.428
retention and disposition; loss
of context; trustworthiness;

00:47:41.428 --> 00:47:44.292
jurisdiction; and ethics.

00:47:44.292 --> 00:47:47.238
So, traditionally we deal
with this with policies,

00:47:47.238 --> 00:47:48.765
training, model agreements.

00:47:48.765 --> 00:47:49.746
But that's not enough.

00:47:49.746 --> 00:47:51.456
We need much more,

00:47:51.456 --> 00:47:55.623
because many or any copy
can only replace an original

00:47:56.939 --> 00:47:58.616
by having the authority

00:47:58.616 --> 00:48:01.937
and the effectiveness of an original.

00:48:01.937 --> 00:48:04.777
And we don't know that any of this does.

00:48:04.777 --> 00:48:05.944
So stay tuned!

00:48:07.646 --> 00:48:08.479
Thank you.

00:48:08.479 --> 00:48:10.812
(applause)

